VERDICT: FIX findings=6
| ID | sev | file:line | defect (one sentence) | fix (one sentence) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| F1 | high | plan_v1.md:96 (cmd/bd/claim.go row) | Defaulting `--assignee` to the actor helper (BD_ACTOR > $USER > "unknown", cmd/bd/main.go:542-550) makes two concurrent defaulted `bd claim` calls on this single-user host resolve to the same assignee, so the second lands in the RENEW cell and both exit 0 — the exact two-winners race the verb exists to remove — and the plan's E2E and race tests use only explicit distinct assignees so the default path is never exercised. | Make `--assignee` a required flag (usage error, exit 1, when absent) and delete the actor-default from the claim.go row. |
| F2 | high | internal/importer/importer.go:561-587, 656-682 | Import into an existing store applies changes through explicit field maps that omit `claim_expires_at`, so a claim synced via JSONL to a linked clone updates status/assignee but silently drops the lease (leaving the bead `in_progress` with no expiry, hence never stealable there), and Phase 3's "export → import into fresh store" test exercises only the `insertIssue` create path and cannot detect this. | Move importer.go from "Files NOT Affected" into Phase 3: map `claim_expires_at` in both update maps (admitting the field through `allowedUpdateFields`), and extend the round-trip test to import into a store that already contains the issue so the update path is covered. |
| F3 | med | internal/storage/memory/memory.go:188 | Adding `ClaimIssue` to the `storage.Storage` interface (Phase 1 storage.go row) breaks compilation of `MemoryStorage`, which implements the full interface and backs `--no-db` mode (cmd/bd/nodb.go:44), and the Changes table has no row for it. | Add a memory.go row to Phase 1 (mutex-serialized `ClaimIssue` with the same decision matrix, or an explicit not-supported error for `--no-db` mode) so the bead Touches account for the file. |
| F4 | med | internal/storage/sqlite/issues.go:44, 74 | The explicit INSERT column lists that the JSONL-import create path depends on (`insertIssue`/`insertIssues`) live in issues.go, which is absent from the Changes table — the queries.go row claims ownership of the "SELECT/INSERT column lists" — so a bead scoped from the table misses the file and a commit touching it fails Touches validation. | Add an issues.go row to Phase 1 covering both INSERT statements (and note the `pinned` grep spans ~30 files, most needing no change, so the row list, not the grep, is the builder's authority). |
| F5 | med | plan_v1.md:31-34 (decision matrix) | The documented legacy claim idiom `bd update ID --status in_progress` with no assignee (the "Claim" recipe in CLAUDE.md and shared-docs BEADS.md) produces in_progress rows with empty assignee that fall into the DENY cell with no holder to print and no lease to expire, making them permanently unclaimable through the new verb during the migration window. | Define the cell explicitly — `in_progress ∧ assignee == ""` → CLAIM (there is no owner to protect) — and add it to the claim_test outcome matrix. |
| F6 | med | internal/merge/merge.go:133-141, 542-547 | `mergeIssue` rebuilds every both-live row from the modeled struct and `Merge3Way` re-marshals every output row from it, so unmodeled-field drop happens on all rows the driver rewrites, not "on true conflicts" as the plan states, and Phase 3's conflict-only test cases would pass even if the builder models the fields in `merge.Issue` but forgets to copy them into `mergeIssue`'s rebuilt result. | Correct the characterization in the JSONL/merge paragraph and add a no-conflict pass-through test: an issue changed on one side in an unrelated field must retain `assignee` and `claim_expires_at` in the merged output. |

Review written to `work/w3_atomic-claim/review_codex_v1.md`. Everything else in the plan checked out against source: `RunInTransaction` really does `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` on a dedicated connection with busy-timeout retry, migration 027 numbering and the 023 idempotency pattern hold, `ComputeContentHash` covers assignee+status, old-binary rollback is safe (all scanners use explicit column lists), `bd daemon --stop-all` exists, and the rollout window is benign — an old daemon answers `OpClaim` with "unknown operation" (exit 1, not a false success), and the version gate auto-restarts mismatched daemons.
